  and not (depth = 'research' and level = 'advanced' and format = 'read');

with q as (
  select $astroDyn_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Daniel Kirkwood identified the asteroid-belt gaps in 1867, but the mechanism emptying them was not understood for 116 years, and Jack Wisdom supplied it in 1982-83. What did shorter numerical integrations get wrong, and what did Wisdom find?", "options": ["Earlier integrations used the wrong value for Jupiter's mass, so their resonance locations were slightly off; Wisdom corrected the mass and the gaps appeared.", "Earlier integrations ran only thousands of orbits, over which a 3:1 orbit genuinely looks stable with modestly oscillating, bounded eccentricity; Wisdom's faster mapping technique reached millions of years and found the eccentricity evolves chaotically, sitting quiet for perhaps a million years and then jumping abruptly above 0.3, enough to cross Mars's orbit.", "Earlier integrations ignored collisions between asteroids, which are what actually clear the gaps; Wisdom added a collisional term and reproduced the depletion.", "Earlier integrations treated the asteroids as massless, so they felt no mutual gravity; Wisdom gave them mass and found they scattered each other out of the resonances."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The obvious mechanism (resonant perturbations pumping eccentricity until the orbit crosses a planet's) seemed to fail when computers first integrated the orbits: objects placed in the 3:1 were advanced for thousands of orbits and their eccentricities oscillated modestly and stayed bounded, so the orbits looked stable and the gap should not have been there. The error was the length of the run, not its accuracy. Wisdom developed a mapping technique fast enough to reach millions of years and found the eccentricity is chaotic: it stays low for perhaps a million years, then jumps abruptly above 0.3, sufficient to cross Mars, sometimes falling back and sometimes not. Integrate for a thousand orbits and the object looks stable; integrate for a million and it leaves. The general lesson is that a simulation run too short does not give an approximate answer, it gives the wrong answer, because the phenomenon that matters may not have happened yet. Jupiter's mass, mutual asteroid gravity, and collisions are not the issue; the resonant chaos operates on million-year timescales that the early runs never reached."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The single relation a = a_Jupiter times (q/p)^(2/3), from Kepler's third law, locates every mean-motion resonance in the belt. Applied with Jupiter's semi-major axis of 5.2029 AU, what does it predict, and what is the central puzzle in its results?", "options": ["It predicts that all low-integer resonances are gaps, and the puzzle is why a few unexpected asteroids nonetheless survive inside the 3:1 and 5:2.", "It predicts resonance locations only to within about ten percent, and the puzzle is why the observed gaps are so much sharper than the formula's uncertainty allows.", "It predicts the 3:1, 5:2, 7:3 and 2:1 as empty gaps but the 3:2 (Hildas), 4:3 (Thule) and 1:1 (Trojans) as dense concentrations, all matching observation to better than half a percent; the puzzle is that the same planet, mechanism and formula produce empty gaps at some resonances and crowded islands at others.", "It predicts that resonances closer to Jupiter are always fuller than those farther away, and the puzzle is a single exception at the 2:1."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "With p asteroid orbits in the time Jupiter completes q, the period ratio is q/p and Kepler's third law gives a = a_J times (q/p)^(2/3). Evaluated at 5.2029 AU it places the 3:1 at 2.501 AU, 5:2 at 2.825, 7:3 at 2.958 and 2:1 at 3.278 (all Kirkwood gaps), and the 3:2 at 3.971 (Hildas), 4:3 at 4.295 (Thule) and 1:1 at 5.203 (Trojans), all concentrations, every entry matching observation to better than half a percent from an equation with no free parameters. The central puzzle sits in the last column: the same planet, the same mechanism and the same formula produce empty gaps at some resonances and dense crowds at others. That asymmetry, resolved by asking what each resonance's phase-lock protects against, is where the physics actually starts. The formula is accurate to a fraction of a percent, not ten percent, and proximity to Jupiter does not determine fullness; the 1:1 sits right at Jupiter and is full while the 2:1 just inside it is empty."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The 3:1 resonance with Jupiter is an empty gap, yet the Hildas at the 3:2 and Pluto at the 3:2 with Neptune are stable for billions of years. Since all three are low-integer resonances, what distinguishes a destructive resonance from a protective one?", "options": ["A destructive resonance has an odd integer ratio while a protective one has an even ratio, so the parity of p plus q decides the outcome.", "A destructive resonance lets the eccentricity climb with no control over the phase at which the body meets the perturber, so it eventually suffers a close encounter and is ejected (survival about 1-2 Myr in the 3:1); a protective resonance librates so that conjunction always falls near the body's aphelion, guaranteeing a minimum approach distance, so Pluto and Neptune never come within about 17 AU despite crossing orbits.", "A destructive resonance occurs only near a gas giant while a protective one occurs only near an ice giant, so the identity of the perturbing planet decides it.", "A destructive resonance requires the body to have high eccentricity while a protective one requires low eccentricity, so the two never involve orbit-crossing paths."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A resonance is a phase lock, and whether the lock protects or destroys depends on where it places the body when the perturber comes round. At the 3:1 the eccentricity is driven chaotically upward and the resonant angle circulates, so nothing controls the phase at which the asteroid reaches a planet-crossing point; once the eccentricity is high enough it suffers a close encounter and is scattered out, with a survival time of only about one to two million years, hence a gap. At the 3:2 the resonant angle librates, guaranteeing that conjunction with the perturber always occurs near the body's aphelion, far from the planet. This gives a guaranteed minimum approach distance: the Hildas can reach Jupiter's distance yet never come near Jupiter, and Pluto's orbit crosses Neptune's yet the two never approach within about 17 AU, so Pluto survives for billions of years precisely because of the resonance. The parity of the integers, the type of planet, and the eccentricity value are all irrelevant; both protected bodies have substantial eccentricities and orbit-crossing paths. It is the libration geometry that decides the outcome."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The delivery pipeline predicts roughly 1,000 near-Earth objects above 1 km with a 10 Myr median lifetime, implying resupply and a total throughput since formation. How is this steady-state bookkeeping independently checked, and what role do iron meteorites play?", "options": ["It is checked by counting fresh impact craters on the Moon, and iron meteorites calibrate the crater-production rate because they leave the most distinctive craters.", "It is checked by cosmic-ray exposure ages: stony meteorites give 10-50 Myr, matching the predicted transit time (a few Myr drifting to a resonance plus the roughly 10 Myr near-Earth lifetime), while iron meteorites give 100-1,000 Myr and act as a control, showing the difference is a property of the material's toughness rather than of the route.", "It is checked by measuring the belt's total mass with spacecraft flybys, and iron meteorites reveal the primordial mass because they are undifferentiated.", "It is checked by the Yarkovsky drift rate measured on Bennu, and iron meteorites drift fastest because metal has the highest thermal conductivity."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The bookkeeping first: about 1,000 near-Earth objects above 1 km divided by a 10 Myr lifetime gives 100 new objects per Myr, and over the 4,568 Myr age of the solar system roughly 460,000 kilometre-scale bodies have passed through and been destroyed, so the population is a continuously resupplied throughput rather than a primordial leftover (10 Myr is far too short to survive 4.5 Gyr). That argument would be worth little if it could only be checked against itself, so it is tested against cosmic-ray exposure ages, which measure the time since a fragment was chipped off its parent and exposed to cosmic rays in open space. Stony meteorites give 10-50 Myr, matching the pipeline's predicted transit time (a few Myr of Yarkovsky drift to a resonance plus a roughly 10 Myr near-Earth lifetime), and the two disjoint methods, orbital mechanics and nuclear spallation, agree without either being built to reproduce the other. Iron meteorites give far longer ages of 100-1,000 Myr because iron is mechanically tough and survives collisions that shatter stone, so they are the control that shows the difference is a property of the material, not the route, confirming that the stony figure is the one that tests the pipeline."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The cold classical Kuiper Belt (roughly 42 to 47 AU, low inclination and eccentricity, uniformly red, about 30 percent wide binaries) is called the control group for migration models. Why are its fragile wide binaries stronger evidence of an undisturbed history than the low inclinations alone?", "options": ["Because wide binaries are brighter and easier to detect than single objects, so they give more statistically reliable data even though they carry the same information as the inclinations.", "Because a wide binary orbits at only about 2 m/s (two 100-km bodies of density 1000 kg/m3 separated by 20,000 km) while encounter speeds in a stirred population are about 1 km/s, some 500 times greater, so a single encounter would tear the pair apart; their survival rules out any violent episode in the entire past, whereas low inclination could in principle be a stirred population later re-cooled.", "Because the binaries prove the objects are young, and only a recently formed population could still be dynamically cold.", "Because wide binaries can only form in a stirred environment, so their presence directly measures how much stirring the population has experienced."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Compute what a cold classical wide binary is: two bodies of radius 50 km (100 km across) and density 1000 kg/m3, separated by 20,000 km, each about 5.2 times 10 to the 17 kg, orbit each other at v = sqrt(G times 2m over a) of roughly 1.9 m/s, walking pace across a gap twice the width of Earth. A dynamically stirred population has encounter velocities of order 1 km/s, about 500 times that orbital speed, and a single such encounter delivers an impulse vastly exceeding the feeble binding energy and separates the pair immediately. So the binaries cannot survive stirring, not merely are unlikely to. That makes them a far stricter witness than low inclination, which shows only that the population is dynamically cold now and could in principle describe a stirred population that was later re-cooled or damped; a wide binary is destroyed rather than merely heated by a rough history, so its survival rules out any violent episode across the whole past. This is why the cold classicals form a control group: a migration model must be violent enough to strip the belt and capture the Trojans yet gentle enough to leave these binaries intact at 44 AU. The binaries are not merely easier to observe, do not indicate youth, and are not products of stirring."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Jupiter's Trojans number about 13,000, are uniformly dark and red, and divide into two colour groups. Explain in full why their colours make them evidence for planetary migration rather than a curiosity, why 'two matching populations' is stronger than one matching colour, and how the Lucy mission is designed to test the capture hypothesis. Refer to the mission's targets and dates.", "rubric": "A strong answer lays out the inference chain. (1) Surface colour tracks composition and formation environment, and different regions of the disk produced characteristic colours (stony and neutral inner, dark and red in the volatile-rich far outer system). (2) If the Trojans had condensed at Jupiter's distance and settled locally into the L4 and L5 points, they should share the colours of the neighbouring outer main belt (S-types and C-types). (3) Instead they are dark and red, matching Kuiper Belt objects that formed beyond Neptune, so a population parked at 5.2 AU wears the colours of one that lives at about 40 AU. (4) Because a body cannot easily change its intrinsic colour class, the natural reading is that they are outer-system material transported inward and captured into the Lagrange points during an episode when the giant planets were migrating, which is exactly what a Neptune sweeping outward through the primordial disk provides. The answer should explain why two matching populations is much stronger than one matching colour: the Trojans split into a 'less red' and a 'very red' group, and these two groups match the two colour populations found in the Kuiper Belt, so it is not merely 'similar material' but the same source population transported with its internal structure preserved. It should note the scale (about 13,000 catalogued, the true kilometre-scale population plausibly comparable to the whole main belt) so this is not a fluke of small numbers. On Lucy: launched in 2021, it visited the main-belt asteroid Dinkinesh in 2023 (finding the contact-binary satellite Selam), Donaldjohanson in 2025, reaches the Trojans proper from 2027, and its final target in 2033 is Patroclus-Menoetius, a near-equal-mass binary. Credit strong answers for explaining why the binary target matters disproportionately: a wide equal-mass binary is hard to form and easy to destroy, so its survival at L5 is a sharp constraint on how violent the capture can have been, and a captured binary bounds the capture impulse from above, eliminating whole classes of capture mechanism. Full credit requires the colour-to-capture chain, the two-populations point, and Lucy's role in testing composition up close rather than from Earth."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The transferable idea of the chapter is that 'a mechanism that explains everything explains nothing, so ask what the model forbids.' Explain how the seven facts across four populations argue for a single migration event, why the honest status of that model is that Neptune's migration is established but its timing and trigger are contested, and how the cold classicals convert the story from a flexible narrative into a constrained theory. Include the recurring role of 'the object that nothing happened to.'", "rubric": "A strong answer assembles the seven facts and pairs the migration event with each: (1) the asteroid belt retains only about 0.03 percent of its expected mass; (2) its composition is radially stirred, with outer-system D-types implanted in the outer belt; (3) Neptune's resonances are heavily populated, including a 2:1 that is a gap at Jupiter; (4) the scattered disc exists; (5) Jupiter's Trojans carry Kuiper Belt colours in two matching groups; (6) the giant planets' spacings and eccentricities are not those of a quiescent disk; (7) Mars is anomalously small. One episode, the giant planets migrating with Neptune sweeping outward and its resonances capturing objects while material is implanted inward (and, in the Grand Tack variant, Jupiter tacking to 1.5 AU and back to address facts 1, 2 and 7), explains all of them at once, and the strength of the argument is that it constrains many independent observables rather than being tuned to one. The answer should state the honest status: it is well established that Neptune migrated outward, because the resonant Kuiper populations (plutinos, twotinos) have no plausible origin other than capture by a sweeping resonance and their excited eccentricities and inclinations match that prediction; but the timing and trigger are contested. The Nice model originally tied the instability to a late event around 700 Myr motivated by a Late Heavy Bombardment spike, and that evidence has weakened because the lunar impact-melt ages clustering near 3.8 to 4.0 Gyr may be an Imbrium-ejecta sampling artefact rather than a system-wide surge, with current work favouring an early instability within the first roughly 100 Myr. The key methodological turn: a mechanism powerful enough to rearrange everything would be unfalsifiable, so the decisive evidence is a population that was NOT disturbed. The cold classicals (42 to 47 AU, low inclination and eccentricity, uniformly red, about 30 percent wide binaries orbiting at under 2 m/s, some 500 times slower than the roughly 1 km/s encounter speeds of a stirred population) cannot have been scattered, so any model must be violent enough to strip the belt and capture the Trojans yet gentle enough to leave those binaries intact at 44 AU, which kills several variants and converts a narrative into a constrained theory. A full answer connects this to the recurring principle that the object that nothing happened to is the one that makes the argument, citing the parallel undisturbed cases (Callisto outside the resonant heating chain, and the iron meteorites whose long exposure ages show the stony ages reflect route not material), because science advances by exclusion and only an undisturbed case can exclude."}]$astroDyn_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/dynamical-architecture', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
